expo-01.answer:
Keep the splash screen on screen until your data (or any other async work) finishes, then hide it manually.

**Steps**

1. **Prevent the splash screen from auto‑hiding** as soon as the app starts.  
   ```tsx
   import * as SplashScreen from 'expo-splash-screen';
   SplashScreen.preventAutoHideAsync();   // keep it visible
   ```

2. **Load your data** (or perform any async initialization) in a `useEffect`. When the work is done, set a “ready” flag.  

3. **Hide the splash screen** once the app is ready.  
   ```tsx
   useEffect(() => {
     if (isReady) {
       SplashScreen.hideAsync();   // or SplashScreen.hide()
     }
   }, [isReady]);
   ```

4. **Render nothing** while waiting, so the splash screen stays visible.  

**Example (without Expo Router)**【3】:

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { View, Text } from 'react-native';
import * as SplashScreen from 'expo-splash-screen';

// Keep the splash screen visible while we fetch resources
SplashScreen.preventAutoHideAsync();

export default function App() {
  const [isReady, setIsReady] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function loadData() {
      try {
        // await your API calls or other async work here
      } catch (e) {
        console.warn(e);
      } finally {
        setIsReady(true);
      }
    }
    loadData();
  }, []);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (isReady) {
      SplashScreen.hideAsync();
    }
  }, [isReady]);

  if (!isReady) return null;   // splash stays visible

  return (
    <View style={{ flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center', alignItems: 'center' }}>
      <Text>App content</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

The splash screen will remain visible while your data loads and disappear as soon as `isReady` becomes `true`.

expo-04.answer:
To read environment variables from a **.env** file in your app code, create a **.env** file in the root of your project directory and prefix your variables with `EXPO_PUBLIC_` [2]. 

You can then access them directly in your JavaScript code using `process.env` [2]:

```tsx
import { Button } from 'react-native';

function Post() {
  const apiUrl = process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL;

  async function onPress() {
    await fetch(apiUrl, {... })
  }

  return <Button onPress={onPress} title="Post" />;
}
```

When you run `npx expo start`, Expo CLI automatically loads these variables and replaces references to `process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_[VARNAME]` with the corresponding value in your app bundle [2].

expo-05.answer:
To protect screens in Expo Router so that only signed-in users can see them, you can use protected routes with `Stack.Protected` (or `Tabs.Protected`) and set the `guard` prop based on your authentication state [2][4]. 

Here is an example setup using a root layout:

```tsx src/app/_layout.tsx
import { Stack } from 'expo-router';
import { useSession } from '@/ctx';

export function RootNavigator() {
  const { session } = useSession();

  return (
    <Stack>
      <Stack.Protected guard={!!session}>
        <Stack.Screen name="(app)" />
      </Stack.Protected>

      <Stack.Protected guard={!session}>
        <Stack.Screen name="sign-in" />
      </Stack.Protected>
    </Stack>
  );
}
```[4]

If a user tries to access a protected screen or deep links into it while unauthenticated, they will automatically be redirected to the next available unprotected screen (such as the sign-in screen) [1][2][4].

expo-09.answer:
**Answer**

Use the foreground‑only request API:

```ts
import * as Location from 'expo-location';

async function askForegroundPermission() {
  const { status } = await Location.requestForegroundPermissionsAsync();
  if (status!== 'granted') {
    // permission denied – handle accordingly
    return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

* `requestForegroundPermissionsAsync` maps to the iOS **When In Use** permission and the Android foreground location permission [1].  
* On iOS you must also have the usage‑description keys in *Info.plist* (e.g., `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`) [7].  
* On Android the required permissions (`ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION` / `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION`) are added automatically when you install `expo-location` [4].

Call this function when the app is active (e.g., on launch or before you start tracking). The request will prompt the user, and the returned `status` tells you whether the permission was granted.

expo-18.answer:
To use `useSQLiteContext()`, wrap your component tree in an `SQLiteProvider` and then call `useSQLiteContext()` inside a child component [1][2].

Here is an example based on the documentation:

```tsx
import { SQLiteProvider, useSQLiteContext, type SQLiteDatabase } from 'expo-sqlite';
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

export default function App() {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <SQLiteProvider databaseName="test.db">
        <Header />
      </SQLiteProvider>
    </View>
  );
}

export function Header() {
  const db = useSQLiteContext();
  const [version, setVersion] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    async function setup() {
      const result = await db.getFirstAsync<{ 'sqlite_version()': string }>(
        'SELECT sqlite_version()'
      );
      setVersion(result?.['sqlite_version()']?? '');
    }
    setup();
  }, [db]);

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>SQLite version: {version}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center', alignItems: 'center' },
});
```

You can also integrate `SQLiteProvider` with `React.Suspense` by passing the `useSuspense` prop [1]:

```tsx
<Suspense fallback={<Text>Loading...</Text>}>
  <SQLiteProvider databaseName="test.db" useSuspense>
    <Header />
  </SQLiteProvider>
</Suspense>
```

vs-1-v58.answer:
RTL support is enabled by default in SDK 58 and later [5]. To explicitly configure or opt into RTL layout support using the `expo-localization` config plugin in your **app.json**, use the `supportsRTL` or `forcesRTL` options [4][5]:

```json app.json
{
  "expo": {
    "plugins": [
      [
        "expo-localization",
        {
          "supportsRTL": true,
          "forcesRTL": false
        }
      ]
    ]
  }
}
```

* **`supportsRTL`**: Set to `true` (default) to allow the app to render in RTL for RTL device languages following React Native's `I18nManager`, or `false` to opt out [4][5].
* **`forcesRTL`**: Set to `true` if you want to force RTL layout regardless of the device language (useful for testing or apps localized only for RTL locales) [4][5].

vs-1-v57.answer:
To turn on RTL layout with the **expo‑localization** config plugin, add the plugin to your app config and set the `supportsRTL` option to `true` (the default is already enabled, but you can declare it explicitly).

```json
// app.json
{
  "expo": {
    "plugins": [
      [
        "expo-localization",
        {
          "supportsRTL": true   // enable RTL layout
        }
      ]
    ]
  }
}
```

After rebuilding the app (e.g., with EAS Build or `expo prebuild`), the navigation and UI will follow the device’s RTL setting automatically.  

*Supported by the expo‑localization config plugin*【2】.

vs-2-v58.answer:
To mark copied clipboard content as sensitive on Android, set `android.isSensitive: true` when calling `setStringAsync` or `setImageAsync` [1]. 

Here is an example for both sensitive text and images:

```ts
import * as Clipboard from 'expo-clipboard';

// Sensitive text
await Clipboard.setStringAsync('my-secret-token', {
  android: { isSensitive: true },
});

// Sensitive image
await Clipboard.setImageAsync(base64Image, {
  android: { isSensitive: true },
});
```

rn-04.answer:
Use the **`onChangeText`** prop of `TextInput`. It is called each time the user types, receiving the new text as a plain string.

```tsx
import React, {useState} from 'react';
import {TextInput, View} from 'react-native';

export default function Example() {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('');

  return (
    <View>
      <TextInput
        value={value}
        onChangeText={text => setValue(text)}   // runs on every keystroke
        placeholder="Type here"
        style={{borderWidth: 1, padding: 8}}
      />
    </View>
  );
}
```

`onChangeText` “Callback that is called when the text input's text changes. Changed text is passed as a single string argument to the callback handler.”【4】

